<!DOCTYPE html><html lang="en"> <head><script>
      // Light is the default; only a saved "dark" choice flips it. Runs before
      // first paint so there is no theme flash.
      (function () {
        try {
          if (localStorage.getItem('ocdc-theme') === 'dark') {
            document.documentElement.setAttribute('data-theme', 'dark');
          }
        } catch (e) {}
      })();
      function toggleTheme() {
        var root = document.documentElement;
        var next = root.getAttribute('data-theme') === 'dark' ? 'light' : 'dark';
        if (next === 'dark') { root.setAttribute('data-theme', 'dark'); }
        else { root.removeAttribute('data-theme'); }
        try { localStorage.setItem('ocdc-theme', next); } catch (e) {}
      }
    </script><meta charset="UTF-8"><meta name="viewport" content="width=device-width, initial-scale=1.0"><link rel="icon" type="image/svg+xml" href="/favicon.svg"><title>How to Move Ollama Models to Another Drive (macOS, Linux, Windows)</title><meta name="description" content="Set OLLAMA_MODELS and move your model library off a full boot drive. The official steps per operating system, the systemd drop-in written out, and the macOS gotcha the docs do not mention: launchctl setenv does not survive a reboot."><meta name="keywords" content="move ollama models to another drive, OLLAMA_MODELS, ollama models directory, change ollama storage location, ollama models external drive, ollama systemd environment, ollama disk full"><meta name="robots" content="index, follow"><link rel="canonical" href="https://openclawdc.com/blog/move-ollama-models-another-drive/"><meta property="og:title" content="How to Move Ollama Models to Another Drive (macOS, Linux, Windows)"><meta property="og:description" content="Set OLLAMA_MODELS and move your model library off a full boot drive. The official steps per operating system, the systemd drop-in written out, and the macOS gotcha the docs do not mention: launchctl setenv does not survive a reboot."><meta property="og:type" content="article"><meta property="og:url" content="https://openclawdc.com/blog/move-ollama-models-another-drive/"><meta property="og:image" content="https://openclawdc.com/images/og-image.jpg"><meta property="og:locale" content="en_US"><meta name="twitter:card" content="summary_large_image"><meta name="twitter:title" content="How to Move Ollama Models to Another Drive (macOS, Linux, Windows)"><meta name="twitter:description" content="Set OLLAMA_MODELS and move your model library off a full boot drive. The official steps per operating system, the systemd drop-in written out, and the macOS gotcha the docs do not mention: launchctl setenv does not survive a reboot."><script type="application/ld+json">{"@context":"https://schema.org","@graph":[{"@type":"Organization","@id":"https://openclawdc.com/#organization","name":"OpenClaw DC","url":"https://openclawdc.com/","logo":"https://openclawdc.com/images/og-image.jpg","email":"openclaw@saurav.io","founder":{"@type":"Person","name":"Saurav","url":"https://cloudyeti.io"},"sameAs":["https://www.youtube.com/@CloudYeti"]},{"@type":"WebSite","@id":"https://openclawdc.com/#website","name":"OpenClaw DC","url":"https://openclawdc.com/","description":"The independent guide to OpenClaw — installation, troubleshooting, model picks, cost optimization, and ecosystem comparisons.","publisher":{"@id":"https://openclawdc.com/#organization"}}]}</script><script type="application/ld+json">{"@context":"https://schema.org","@type":"TechArticle","headline":"How to Move Ollama Models to Another Drive","author":{"@type":"Organization","name":"OpenClaw DC","url":"https://openclawdc.com"},"datePublished":"2026-08-25","dateModified":"2026-08-25","publisher":{"@type":"Organization","name":"OpenClaw DC","url":"https://openclawdc.com"}}</script>
<script type="application/ld+json">{"@context":"https://schema.org","@type":"FAQPage","mainEntity":[{"@type":"Question","name":"Where does Ollama store its models?","acceptedAnswer":{"@type":"Answer","text":"The default directory depends on the operating system. On macOS it is ~/.ollama/models. On Linux, installed with the standard installer, it is /usr/share/ollama/.ollama/models. On Windows it is C:\\Users\\%username%\\.ollama\\models. Inside that directory you will find two subdirectories, blobs and manifests. The blobs hold the actual weight data and the manifests describe which blobs make up each tagged model, so both must move together."}},{"@type":"Question","name":"How do I change where Ollama stores models?","acceptedAnswer":{"@type":"Answer","text":"Set the environment variable OLLAMA_MODELS to the directory you want, then restart Ollama. On macOS run launchctl setenv OLLAMA_MODELS \"/your/path\" and restart the app. On Linux run systemctl edit ollama.service, add Environment=\"OLLAMA_MODELS=/your/path\" under the [Service] section, then run systemctl daemon-reload and systemctl restart ollama. On Windows, quit Ollama from the taskbar, open Edit environment variables for your account, create OLLAMA_MODELS with your path, and restart Ollama. Move your existing blobs and manifests directories to the new location before you restart, or Ollama will start with an empty library."}},{"@type":"Question","name":"Why did my OLLAMA_MODELS setting stop working after a reboot?","acceptedAnswer":{"@type":"Answer","text":"On macOS, because launchctl setenv is not persistent. It sets the variable for the current boot only, so the value is lost when the system restarts and Ollama returns to the default directory. To make it permanent, create a LaunchAgent plist in ~/Library/LaunchAgents that runs the same launchctl setenv command at login. On Linux the equivalent mistake is exporting the variable in a shell profile: a shell export never reaches a systemd-managed daemon, so you must use a systemd drop-in instead."}},{"@type":"Question","name":"Why can Ollama not write to my new models directory on Linux?","acceptedAnswer":{"@type":"Answer","text":"Because the standard Linux installer runs the daemon as a dedicated ollama user, and that user needs read and write access to the directory you chose. The documented fix is sudo chown -R ollama:ollama followed by the directory path. Check the parent directories as well, because the ollama user also needs execute permission on every directory in the path to traverse into it. A mount point owned by root with restrictive permissions will block the daemon even when the target directory itself is correct."}},{"@type":"Question","name":"Can I keep Ollama models on an external drive?","acceptedAnswer":{"@type":"Answer","text":"Yes, and the interface speed is what decides whether it is pleasant. USB4 and Thunderbolt run at 40 Gbps while USB 3.2 Gen 2 runs at 10 Gbps, so a USB4 NVMe enclosure loads models at close to internal speeds and a 10 Gbps enclosure is several times slower. The real risk is not speed but availability: if the drive is not mounted when the service starts, Ollama sees an empty or unwritable directory. Treat an external drive as an archive tier and keep the models you run daily on internal storage."}}]}</script>
<script type="application/ld+json">{"@context":"https://schema.org","@type":"BreadcrumbList","itemListElement":[{"@type":"ListItem","position":1,"name":"Home","item":"https://openclawdc.com/"},{"@type":"ListItem","position":2,"name":"Blog","item":"https://openclawdc.com/blog/"},{"@type":"ListItem","position":3,"name":"How to Move Ollama Models to Another Drive (macOS, Linux, Windows)","item":"https://openclawdc.com/blog/move-ollama-models-another-drive/"}]}</script><!-- Google tag (gtag.js) --><script async src="https://www.googletagmanager.com/gtag/js?id=G-M0BK28XHM9"></script><script>
